From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: irisrun <irisrun@localhost>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 07:42:04 +0700
Subject: [PATCH 36/37] Embed UIKit views and view controllers with
 representables on Linux

UIViewRepresentable and UIViewControllerRepresentable now work with Iris's
UIKit, with the lifecycle of SwiftUI's own code, opened for Linux: the
coordinator first, then makeUIView and updateUIView, updateUIView again as
the view's inputs or the environment it reads change, and
dismantleUIView once the view goes; sizeThatFits(_:uiView:context:), or
else the view's intrinsic size and content priorities, size it.

As on iOS, the view sits in a PlatformViewHost, a subview of the hosting
view, which sizes it to its bounds, tells SwiftUI when its intrinsic size
changes, and makes a view controller a child of the hosting controller
while it is in a window. The layer renderer finds the display list's
platform views and leaves a layer at their places among SwiftUI's
layers, which their own layers draw in, so that SwiftUI content drawn
over them stays in front and their opacity, clips and transforms apply;
the hosting view gives them the matching frames in UIKit.

A representable's view has a responder among SwiftUI's, so the hosting
view asks SwiftUI's hit-testing which side a touch goes to: into the
UIKit view, hit-tested there as UIKit does, unless SwiftUI content in
front of it takes the touch.
